    51	# Accumulation Precision
    52	Ops like matmul or those that compute statistics like GroupNorm or LayerNorm accumulate intermediate results en route to a final value. The precision in which these intermediate values are computed and (if needed) stored should be sufficiently high to not accumulate large errors during the running calculation. Typically, this means accumulating in a higher precision than the data type (for data formats with less precision than FP32). Failing to do this can lead to rapidly-deteriorating accuracy as seen in the gray, red (under gray), and orange lines in this plot of matmul accuracy vs. inner dimension length for bfloat16 input data (the pink line is under the greenish-yellow line):
    53	
    54	<img src="images/matmul_acc_vs_k.png" style="width:600px;"/>
    55	
    56	Most ops contain an optional `DeviceComputeKernelConfig` struct argument, which has a member `fp32_dest_acc_en` (for Wormhole and Blackhole), which enables accumulation in FP32 precision. This flag configures the destination registers to operate in FP32 precision when calling the compute APIs from `compute_kernel_api`.
    57	
    58	However, if a kernel needs to store intermediate results to a CB, then that CB must be configured to be able to copy FP32 data from L1 into the destination registers. Otherwise, the data will be converted to TF32 before being copied to dest, gradually eroding the accuracy during the reduction. Configuring the CB requires:
    59	
    60	1. Set `UnpackToDestMode=UnpackToDestFp32` for the intermediate CB in the program setup. For example, to configure a CB with id `cb_id` to be copied from L1 into dest in full FP32 precision, the following must be in the program setup:
    61	```cpp
    62	std::vector<tt::tt_metal::UnpackToDestMode> unpack_to_dest_mode(NUM_CIRCULAR_BUFFERS, tt::tt_metal::UnpackToDestMode::Default);
    63	unpack_to_dest_mode[cb_id] = tt::tt_metal::UnpackToDestMode::UnpackToDestFp32;
    64	
    65	auto compute_kernel = CreateKernel(
    66	    program,
    67	    "my_compute_kernel_file.cpp",
    68	    all_cores,
    69	    tt::tt_metal::ComputeConfig{
    70	        .math_fidelity = math_fidelity,
    71	        .fp32_dest_acc_en = fp32_dest_acc_en,
    72	        .unpack_to_dest_mode = unpack_to_dest_mode, <--- need to pass it into ComputeConfig{}
    73	        .math_approx_mode = math_approx_mode,
    74	        .compile_args = compute_args,
    75	        .defines = compute_defines});
    76	```
    77	This will allow `cb_id` to be unpacked directly into dest without going through the truncation of moving through srcA/B when `copy_tile()` is invoked on that CB (`copy_tile_init(cb_id)` must be called before `copy_tile()`).  <span style="color: red;">Note that this means that `cb_id` can not be used in operations that use the srcA/B registers (e.g. `add_tiles()`).</span> The results must be copied to a CB that is able to use srcA/B operations.
    78	
    79	Failing to configure the accumulator for FP32 accumulation can lead to large, noisy errors as seen in the following plots of 90th percentile ULP error vs. tensor width for LayerNorm. At ~W=2500, the compute kernel switches from one that doesn't use an intermediate accumulator to one that does.
    80	
    81	Without configuring the intermediate accumulator to use FP32 copying, the error is discontinuous across the kernel switch (orange line): \
    82	<img src="images/ln_no_fp32_acc.png" style="width:600px;"/>
    83	
    84	After enabling FP32 copying for the accumulator, the results are smooth across the boundary:
    85	<img src="images/ln_with_fp32_acc.png" style="width:600px;"/>
    86	
    87	Note that using a low-precision accumulator (bfloat16, green line) is completely insufficient to maintain accuracy.
    88	
    89	# Order of Operations
    90	When taking the mean of many values, many ops invoke the `reduce_tile()` API with a scalar tile of $1/N$ values, where $N$ is the number of entries in the mean. This computes the mean via divide-then-sum:
    91	
    92	$$\mu=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac{x_i}{N}$$
    93	
    94	which does $N$ floating-point divisions and $N-1$ floating-point summations, each of these introducing rounding error.
    95	
    96	If the order of operations is switched to sum-then-divide:
    97	
    98	$$\mu=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}x_i$$
    99	
   100	then there are still $N-1$ summations, but only 1 division (at the end). This avoids $N-1$ rounding errors from the extraneous divisions.
   101	
   102	In addition to accumulating division errors, divide-then-sum also risks flushing small numbers to 0. By accumulating then dividing at the end, the accumulator is allowed to grow to a large number before dividing, mitigating the risk of creating small numbers that flush to 0. However, one should also be aware of the risk of overflow if the accumulator grows too large for the data format to handle. This is another reason why accumulation in FP32 precision is desirable, as it has ample dynamic range to handle most problems.
   103	
   104	The effect of this change is less pronounced than using FP32 accumulators, but is still an improvement as it smooths out the oscillations in the error. Compare divide-then-sum (orange line):
   105	
   106	<img src="images/ln_with_fp32_acc.png" style="width:600px;"/>
   107	
   108	to sum-then-divide:
   109	
   110	<img src="images/ln_sum_then_divide.png" style="width:600px;"/>
